> The items are answered on a 6-point scale, so I think it can be treated as continous data?

Well, Rhemtulla et al. (2012) indicated that with 5 categories or more, factor correlations are well estimated when treating the data as continuous, but that is not always the case with factor loadings.  You should also take into account how symmetric your thresholds are (click this link <http://psych.colorado.edu/~willcutt/pdfs/Rhemtulla_2012.pdf> to read the suggestions in the article).

If you treat the data as categorical, you can read about how to do so for multiple groups at this link <http://sunthud.com/catInvariance.html> (including lavaan syntax examples).  Instead of making the constraints across groups, you make constraints across factors in the same 1-group model.  The same way that methods for testing invariance between Groups 1 and 2 generalize to testing invariance between the same factor(s) measured at Times 1 and 2, you simply have the same factor(s) measured for husbands and wives.

> Has someone information how to do this/how to specify the models?

It sounds like you already provided enough information to specify your models.  Can you ask a more specific question?  If you need to know lavaan syntax, you can read the tutorial pages <http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/index.html> and the article <https://www.jstatsoft.org/article/view/v048i02>.  If you want to know more about specifying models to test "longitudinal" (in your case, dyadic) measurement invariance, you can search the archives at SEMNET <http://www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html> or even post your question there.

Terry

