> In reading the message boards here I feel like it's implied that for a bifactor model to be identified the specific factors must be specified as uncorrelated with each other, as well as the general factor.  Is this the case?  That doesn't seem to be how bifactor models are described in the literature (e.g., Kline 2016 p320), but I am certainly no expert.

I think it is necessary when you have few indicators for each method/specific factor, but you might have enough indicators of each factor that you could probably freely estimate all factor covariances - but I'm not sure.  This isn't specific to lavaan, so it's probably better to post it on SEMNET <http://www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html>.  There has been a lot of recent discussion on bifactor models, so try searching the archives first.

